{"id": 61, "ts": 1779835552.3125935, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 4, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I appreciate Anna\u2019s focus on Candidate\u202fC\u2019s reliability and rapid, correct decision\u2011making, which are indeed core to long\u2011distance operations. My own notes confirm that C \u201ckeeps calm in a crisis\u201d and \u201ccan make correct decisions quickly,\u201d reinforcing his strength in stress resilience and decision quality. The concerns you raised\u2014difficulty communicating ideas, an egocentric style, and a lack of willingness to pursue further education\u2014are real, yet when we compare them with the alternatives, the trade\u2011offs become clearer. Candidate\u202fB, while very conscientious and good at assessing weather, \u201cmakes nasty remarks about his colleagues,\u201d which directly threatens crew cooperation and professional communication. Candidate\u202fD, although taking responsibility seriously and solving problems extremely well, \u201chas a hot temper\u201d and is \u201cregarded as a know\u2011it\u2011all,\u201d both of which can erode cockpit harmony. Candidate\u202fA shows strong situational anticipation and leadership but is \u201cregarded as a show\u2011off\u201d and \u201cnot open to new ideas,\u201d raising doubts about adaptability and teamwork. In my view, C\u2019s communication and attitude issues, though important, are less likely to compromise immediate safety than the overt interpersonal conflicts evident in B and D, or the rigidity seen in A. Therefore, I continue to support Candidate\u202fC as the best overall fit, while recommending targeted coaching on communication and openness to development."}
