{"id": 84, "ts": 1779835720.278975, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 5, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I agree with Agent\u202f3 that Candidate\u202fB remains the strongest overall fit, but I want to highlight two additional concerns that affect key safety criteria.\u202fFrom my notes, B has a **relatively weak memory for numbers**, which raises doubts about his attention to detail and information accuracy\u2014critical for flight\u2011deck calculations and checklist compliance.\u202fMoreover, the panel now knows that B can be **grumpy, uncooperative, and occasionally uses the wrong tone**, which directly impacts crew\u2011cooperation and professional communication.\u202fWhile these issues are serious, the alternative candidates each have more fundamental gaps:\u202fCandidate\u202fA lacks evidence of reliability or stress resilience; Candidate\u202fC, despite good decision\u2011making, is egocentric and struggles to communicate ideas; Candidate\u202fD, although technically strong, is described as a know\u2011it\u2011all with a hot temper, which could be equally disruptive.\u202fGiven B\u2019s clear strengths in operational reliability (very conscientious), stress resilience, and technical competence, I still vote for **Candidate\u202fB**, recommending that we address his communication style and memory for numbers through targeted coaching and a brief competency check before final hiring."}
